BANKS["Embedded C"] = [

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-volatile
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 1, q: "What does volatile tell the compiler?", o: ["The variable is shared between threads and must be locked", "Re-read from memory on every access, and do every write", "The variable lives in flash and cannot be cached in RAM", "The variable must be kept in a register for speed"], a: 1, why: "<p>Ordinary C assumes memory only changes when your program changes it. That assumption lets the compiler keep a value in a register across a loop, drop a read it believes is redundant, or delete a write nobody reads back.</p><p>volatile withdraws that assumption for one object. Every read becomes a real load, every write a real store, and their relative order is preserved.</p><p>It does not lock anything, does not imply atomicity, and has nothing to do with where the variable is stored.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 2, q: "A poll loop on a status register works at -O0 and hangs at -O2. Why?", o: ["The optimiser reordered the peripheral clock setup", "The read was hoisted out: it is not volatile", "The loop needs a memory barrier the optimiser removed", "The compiler deleted the loop as having no side effect"], a: 1, why: "<p>At -O2 the compiler analyses the loop body, finds nothing that could change the memory being polled, and concludes the value is loop-invariant. It reads once and emits an unconditional branch to itself.</p><p>Debug builds do not perform that analysis, which is why the same source works at -O0. The debug-works-release-hangs pattern is the signature of this fault and of undefined behaviour generally.</p><p>A barrier would not help, because the problem is not ordering: the read simply never happens again.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 2, q: "Which declares a read-only 32-bit hardware register at a fixed address?", o: ["const uint32_t *reg;", "volatile uint32_t *reg;", "volatile const uint32_t * const reg;", "const volatile uint32_t * volatile reg;"], a: 2, why: "<p>Three qualifiers, each doing a separate job. volatile because the peripheral updates it without your involvement. const on the pointee because writing it is forbidden. const on the pointer because it never points anywhere else.</p><p>Option 1 has no volatile, so the read can be optimised away. Option 2 permits writes to a read-only register. Option 4 protects only the pointer.</p><p>The initialiser also needs a cast carrying the same qualifiers: (volatile const uint32_t *)0x40021000.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 2, q: "What is the difference between volatile int *p and int * volatile p?", o: ["They are identical; the position of volatile is style", "The first qualifies the pointee, the second the pointer", "The second is not valid C: volatile cannot follow a star", "The first cannot be reassigned once it is initialised"], a: 1, why: "<p>Read outward from the identifier. Whatever sits to the left of the star qualifies the thing pointed at; whatever sits to the right of the star, just before the name, qualifies the pointer itself.</p><p>The first is the common case: a hardware register accessed through a pointer you may move. The second is rare and means the pointer variable itself can change unexpectedly, for instance if an ISR reassigns it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 3, q: "Does volatile make reg |= (1u << 3) safe against an interrupt?", o: ["Yes, that is exactly what volatile is there for", "No, it is still read, modify, write", "Only on 32-bit targets, where the access is one word", "Only if the register is 8 bits and the core is 8-bit"], a: 1, why: "<p>volatile guarantees the accesses happen and are not reordered relative to each other. It says nothing about them being indivisible.</p><p>reg |= x compiles to a load, an OR and a store. An interrupt arriving between the load and the store will have its own change to that register silently overwritten when your stale value is written back.</p><p>The fix is a critical section, an atomic operation, or hardware that provides set and clear registers so no read is needed.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 3, q: "Is marking a DMA receive buffer volatile sufficient to read it safely?", o: ["Yes, that is what volatile is for on a DMA buffer", "No: you also need a barrier and a cache invalidate", "Yes, provided the buffer is aligned to a cache line", "Only if the DMA runs single-shot rather than circular"], a: 1, why: "<p>Three separate things can leave the CPU and the DMA controller disagreeing, and volatile addresses only the first. The compiler caching a value in a register: volatile fixes this. A data cache holding a stale line while DMA wrote actual RAM: needs an explicit invalidate. A write buffer holding your stores: needs a barrier.</p><p>Meanwhile marking a whole buffer volatile prevents the compiler optimising any access to it, so every byte of your processing loop becomes a separate load.</p><p>The correct pattern is volatile on the completion flag, a barrier, and cache maintenance on parts that have a cache.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 1, q: "Which of these genuinely needs volatile?", o: ["A loop counter local to a function that never returns", "A const lookup table held in flash and only read", "A flag written by an ISR and polled in main", "A struct passed by value into a nested call"], a: 2, why: "<p>The test is whether the object can change outside the visible flow of the code the compiler is looking at. An ISR runs between arbitrary instructions, so the compiler cannot see the write.</p><p>A local counter changes only where the compiler can see. A const table never changes. A struct passed by value is a copy.</p><p>The full list of genuine cases: memory-mapped registers, ISR-shared variables, signal handler variables, and memory written by another core or a debugger.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 2, q: "Why is the cast needed in (volatile uint32_t *)0x40021000?", o: ["To silence a warning that is otherwise harmless", "C does not let an integer initialise a pointer", "To force the access to be four-byte aligned", "To place the object in flash rather than RAM"], a: 1, why: "<p>Assigning an integer constant to a pointer is a constraint violation, not merely a style issue. GCC reports it as 'initialization of pointer from integer without a cast' and with -Werror the build fails.</p><p>The cast must also carry the same qualifiers as the target type. Casting to (const uint32_t *) and assigning to a volatile uint32_t * produces a second diagnostic about discarding qualifiers.</p><p>The reliable habit: copy the type from the left of the equals sign and append a star.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 3, q: "A delay loop stops working when the build is optimised. Best fix?", o: ["Mark the loop counter volatile so it is not removed", "Compile that one file at -O0 so the loop survives", "Poll the device's own ready bit, with a timeout", "Make the loop longer so it still delays at -O2"], a: 2, why: "<p>Marking the counter volatile does work, and it leaves you with a delay calibrated to one clock speed and one compiler. Change either and the timing silently changes with it.</p><p>Compiling that file at -O0 hides the fault rather than fixing it, and the same class of bug will still be present elsewhere.</p><p>Polling a status bit is correct independent of clock frequency, optimisation level and compiler version, and it returns as soon as the device is genuinely ready rather than after a worst-case wait.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-volatile", d: 2, q: "Two accesses to different volatile objects: what does the standard guarantee?", o: ["They are atomic", "Their relative order is preserved", "They complete before any non-volatile access", "Nothing at all"], a: 1, why: "<p>Accesses to volatile objects are part of the abstract machine's observable behaviour, so the compiler may not reorder them with respect to one another.</p><p>What it does not give you: atomicity, any ordering guarantee against surrounding non-volatile accesses, or any effect on hardware-level reordering such as a write buffer or an out-of-order core.</p><p>This is exactly why volatile is not a substitute for a memory barrier on anything more sophisticated than a simple Cortex-M.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-types
{ t: "emb-types", d: 1, q: "What does the C standard guarantee about the size of int?", o: ["Exactly 32 bits", "Exactly 16 bits", "At least 16 bits", "The same as a pointer"], a: 2, why: "<p>The standard sets a minimum range, not a width. int is 16 bits on many 8 and 16-bit targets and 32 bits on ARM, and nothing guarantees it matches a pointer.</p><p>The practical consequence is that overflow points, bit positions and struct offsets all move when code is ported, which for anyone maintaining drivers across silicon families is routine rather than exceptional.</p><p>Use the fixed-width types from stdint.h for anything touching hardware, a protocol or a struct layout.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 2, q: "uint8_t a = 0xFF; is (~a == 0x00) true?", o: ["Yes, a is unsigned so ~a is 0x00", "No, a promotes to int so ~a is 0xFFFFFF00", "Only on 8-bit targets, where int is also 8 bits", "It is undefined behaviour to complement it"], a: 1, why: "<p>Integer promotion converts anything narrower than int to int before an operator is applied. So a becomes 0x000000FF, and ~a is 0xFFFFFF00, which is not zero.</p><p>The rule is not special to complement: it applies to every operator. It is why uint8_t x = 200, y = 100; x + y gives 300 rather than wrapping to 44.</p><p>If you want byte behaviour, mask back down explicitly: (~a &amp; 0xFF).</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 2, q: "uint8_t x = 200, y = 100; does (x + y) wrap to 44?", o: ["Yes, uint8_t arithmetic wraps round at 255", "No, both promote to int so the result is 300", "Only if the result is stored back into a uint8_t", "It is undefined behaviour, being unsigned overflow"], a: 1, why: "<p>There is no such thing as uint8_t arithmetic. Both operands promote to int, the addition happens in int, and the result is 300.</p><p>Wraparound occurs only at the point of storing back into a narrow type: uint8_t z = x + y; gives 44. So whether you see wrapping depends on where the value lands, not on the types of the operands.</p><p>This catches people writing overflow checks: if (x + y &gt; 255) is true here, which is often not what the author expected.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 3, q: "int len = -1; if (len > 64) return; memcpy(buf, src, len); What happens?", o: ["The check catches it and the function returns", "The check passes and memcpy copies about 4 GB", "The compiler rejects the signed to size_t conversion", "memcpy copies nothing, since the length is negative"], a: 1, why: "<p>The comparison is between two signed ints, so -1 &gt; 64 is false and the guard passes. memcpy's third parameter is size_t, which is unsigned, so -1 converts to 4294967295.</p><p>The shape to recognise is <b>signed check, unsigned use</b>: the validation happens in arithmetic where a negative value looks small and safe, and the use happens in arithmetic where it is enormous. It is behind a long list of real CVEs.</p><p>The fix is to test for negative explicitly before any comparison against a size, or better, not to let a length be signed at all.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 2, q: "On ARM GCC, char c = 0xFF; is (c == 0xFF) true?", o: ["Yes, plain char is unsigned on ARM", "No, plain char is signed on every ARM toolchain", "It is undefined behaviour to compare them", "Only in C99; C11 changed the signedness rule"], a: 0, why: "<p>Plain char has implementation-defined signedness. The ARM procedure call standard specifies unsigned, so c holds 255, promotes to 255, and the comparison is true.</p><p>On x86 with GCC plain char is signed, so c holds -1, promotes to -1, and the same comparison is false. Identical source, identical compiler, opposite behaviour.</p><p>This is a genuine porting hazard and one of the first things to check when a driver moved between targets starts producing wrong values.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 1, q: "How many distinct character types does C have?", o: ["One: char, with an optional signed qualifier", "Two: char and unsigned char, which alias", "Three: char, signed char and unsigned char", "Four, counting wchar_t as a character type"], a: 2, why: "<p>Plain char is a type in its own right, distinct from both signed char and unsigned char, even though it behaves like one of them.</p><p>That is why you cannot assume anything about the sign of a plain char, and why code that needs a specific signedness must say so.</p><p>Reserve plain char for actual text and use uint8_t for byte data. That single habit removes the whole class of problem.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 2, q: "Best way to assemble a signed 16-bit value from two bytes, low byte first?", o: ["*(int16_t *)buf", "(int16_t)((uint16_t)buf[1] << 8 | buf[0])", "(int16_t)(buf[1] << 8 | buf[0]), signed shift", "(int16_t)((uint16_t)buf[0] << 8 | buf[1])"], a: 1, why: "<p>Option 2 builds the value as unsigned, keeping it in range, then casts once at the end.</p><p>Option 1 has three faults at once: it assumes the CPU's byte order matches the sensor's, it may perform an unaligned access, and it violates strict aliasing, which at -O2 permits the compiler to reorder or discard accesses.</p><p>Option 3 assembles directly into a signed type, and converting an out-of-range value to a signed type is implementation-defined above 0x7FFF. Option 4 works but reads as arithmetic rather than as byte assembly, and hides the byte order.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 2, q: "Which type should hold a length or a count?", o: ["int", "size_t", "long", "uint8_t"], a: 1, why: "<p>size_t is unsigned and guaranteed large enough to represent the size of any object, which is precisely what a length is.</p><p>Using a signed type invites the negative-length bug the moment it meets an unsigned API such as memcpy or malloc, because the conversion turns a small negative number into a very large positive one.</p><p>The cost is that you cannot use -1 as an error marker, which is a feature: it forces the error to be reported through a separate return value where a caller must look at it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 3, q: "What is uint_fast8_t for?", o: ["Exactly 8 bits, with faster access than uint8_t", "At least 8 bits, in whatever width is fastest", "8 bits, guaranteed to be held in a register", "A deprecated alias kept for C99 compatibility"], a: 1, why: "<p>The fast types trade an exact width for speed. On a 32-bit core uint_fast8_t is usually 32 bits, because loading and masking a byte costs more than using a full word.</p><p>Use them for loop counters and local flags where only the range matters. Never use them for anything touching hardware, a struct layout or a protocol, because the width is not fixed and will change when you port.</p><p>There is also uint_least8_t, which is the smallest type with at least that range, for when memory matters more than speed.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-types", d: 1, q: "Where is plain char the right choice?", o: ["Byte buffers from a sensor", "Bit flags", "Actual text and strings", "Register values"], a: 2, why: "<p>Plain char exists for characters, and the standard library's string functions take it. That is its job.</p><p>For everything else its implementation-defined signedness makes it a liability: a byte buffer read as signed char produces negative values above 127, which then sign-extend on promotion and give you 0xFFFFFF80 where you expected 0x80.</p><p>Use uint8_t for bytes, uint8_t or a named enum for flags, and the fixed-width types for register values.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-memory
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 1, q: "static uint8_t buf[4096]; with no initialiser costs what?", o: ["4096 bytes of flash", "4096 bytes of RAM", "4096 bytes of both", "Nothing until used"], a: 1, why: "<p>Uninitialised statics go in .bss. They need RAM because they are writable, but nothing needs storing in flash because the initial value is zero and startup simply wipes the region.</p><p>Contrast with an initialised array, which goes in .data and costs both: the values must survive power-off, so they live in flash, and they must be writable, so a copy lives in RAM.</p><p>This asymmetry is why adding const to a lookup table halves what it costs you: const moves it to .rodata, which is flash only.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 2, q: "Why does an initialised global array cost both flash and RAM?", o: ["The compiler duplicates it so reads can be faster", "It must survive power-off and still be writable", "Flash cannot be read at run time by the core", "It does not: an initialised array costs flash only"], a: 1, why: "<p>Two requirements pull in opposite directions. Writable means RAM, because flash cannot be modified byte by byte. Surviving a power cycle means flash, because RAM loses its contents.</p><p>The resolution is to store the initial image in flash and copy it into RAM before main runs. That copy is a real memcpy in the startup file, and you can go and read it.</p><p>You have seen it if you have read an ESP-IDF boot log: segments marked 'load' are being copied to RAM, segments marked 'map' are read in place from flash.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 2, q: "Adding const to a large lookup table does what?", o: ["Nothing: const is only a compile-time check", "Moves it to .rodata, so it costs flash only", "Moves it to .bss, so it costs neither", "Makes each read slower, since flash is slower"], a: 1, why: "<p>A const table is never written, so it needs no writable copy in RAM. It lives in .rodata in flash and is read directly.</p><p>For a 4 kB table on a part with 32 kB of RAM, that one keyword is an eighth of your RAM budget. It is one of the highest-value habits in embedded C.</p><p>It is not slower in any way that usually matters: flash reads go through the same instruction path and are typically cached or prefetched.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 2, q: "Which lives on the stack?", o: ["A global initialised to 5, so it is writable", "A global initialised to 0, which is zeroed at boot", "A const table declared at file scope", "An array declared inside a function"], a: 3, why: "<p>Non-static locals have automatic storage duration: they are allocated when the function is entered and released when it returns.</p><p>The other three are .data, .bss and .rodata respectively. Note that adding static to a local moves it out of the stack and into .bss or .data, which changes its lifetime and makes the function non-reentrant.</p><p>The practical hazard is size: a task stack is often 2 to 4 kB, so an 8 kB local overruns it and corrupts whatever is adjacent, with no error at all.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 3, q: "You add a large global, it links fine, and the board now crashes shortly after boot. Why?", o: ["Flash overflowed and the image is truncated", "The heap shrank, so an allocation now fails", "The array landed in .text and is not writable", "The linker script has the wrong RAM origin"], a: 1, why: "<p>The linker places .data and .bss and checks them against the declared RAM region. Whatever remains becomes the heap at run time, and nothing checks that.</p><p>So adding 8 kB of .bss silently removes 8 kB of heap. Something that previously allocated successfully now fails, and on an RTOS that is usually a task stack, because xTaskCreate takes its stack from the heap.</p><p>If the array had genuinely overflowed the region, the linker would have said 'region RAM overflowed'. That it linked means it fits and the problem is downstream.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 2, q: "In what order does Reset_Handler do its work?", o: ["Call main, then copy .data from flash", "Zero .bss, call main, then copy .data", "Copy .data, zero .bss, constructors, main", "Run constructors, copy .data, zero .bss, then main"], a: 2, why: "<p>The C runtime environment must exist before any C code runs, so both the .data copy and the .bss zeroing come first.</p><p>Static constructors follow, because they are C++ code that may depend on initialised globals. Then main.</p><p>Getting this order wrong is a real bring-up fault: a constructor that runs before .data is copied sees whatever was in RAM at power-on.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 3, q: "On Cortex-M, what happens before the first instruction of Reset_Handler?", o: ["Nothing: Reset_Handler is the first thing to run", "Hardware loads SP from the first vector word", "The bootloader zeroes RAM and sets the stack", "The PLL locks so the core clock is stable"], a: 1, why: "<p>The core reads the first word of the vector table into the main stack pointer, then the second word as the reset vector, and branches there. This is silicon, before any software runs.</p><p>It is unusual to ARM. Most architectures expect the startup code to establish its own stack, which means the first few instructions cannot use one.</p><p>The practical consequence: if the first vector table entry points at RAM that does not exist, the part dies before your code gets a chance, and it looks like a completely dead board.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 2, q: "uint8_t buffer[8192]; declared inside a FreeRTOS task function. What is the risk?", o: ["It is placed in flash and cannot be written", "It overruns a typical 2 to 4 kB task stack", "It cannot be initialised at that size", "It is zeroed on every call, costing time"], a: 1, why: "<p>Locals live on the stack of whichever task is executing. FreeRTOS task stacks are commonly 2 to 4 kB, so an 8 kB local writes straight past the end.</p><p>What makes it dangerous is that there is no error. The overflow scribbles on whatever is next in memory, often another task's control block, and the symptom appears somewhere unrelated and much later.</p><p>Large buffers belong in .bss, in the heap, or in a statically allocated pool. uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark is how you check you have not come close.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 1, q: "Which section holds string literals?", o: [".text", ".rodata", ".data", ".bss"], a: 1, why: "<p>String literals are read-only initialised data, so they sit in flash and are never copied to RAM.</p><p>This is also why modifying a string literal is undefined behaviour: char *p = \"hello\"; p[0] = 'H'; attempts to write flash. Declaring it as const char * makes the compiler catch it.</p><p>Note that char buf[] = \"hello\"; is different: that is an array initialised from a literal, so it lives in .data and is writable.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-memory", d: 3, q: "Your linker reports 'region RAM overflowed'. What does that tell you?", o: ["The heap has been configured too small", "The static sections alone exceed the region", "The stack size in the linker script is too large", "A pointer constant is outside the RAM range"], a: 1, why: "<p>The linker only places and checks .data and .bss. If it errors, those alone do not fit in the declared region.</p><p>The more insidious case is the one that links successfully: .data and .bss fit, and the heap gets whatever is left, which may be too little. Nothing warns you and the failure appears at run time as an allocation that returns null.</p><p>The map file is where you find out which object contributed what, and it is the only tool that answers that question.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-structs
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 2, q: "sizeof(struct { uint8_t a; uint32_t b; uint16_t c; }) on 32-bit ARM?", o: ["7", "8", "12", "16"], a: 2, why: "<p>Work through it byte by byte. a occupies offset 0. b needs 4-byte alignment, so three bytes of padding follow and b sits at offset 4. c sits at offset 8. The struct's own alignment is 4, so the size must be a multiple of 4, giving two bytes of tail padding.</p><p>Total 12, not the 7 the members add up to.</p><p>The tail padding exists so that in an array of these structs, every element's b member is still 4-byte aligned.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 2, q: "Reordering that struct largest-member-first gives what size?", o: ["7", "8", "12", "16"], a: 1, why: "<p>uint32_t at offset 0, uint16_t at 4, uint8_t at 6, then one byte of tail padding to reach a multiple of 4. Total 8.</p><p>Ordering members from largest to smallest generally eliminates internal padding, because each member's alignment requirement is satisfied by the position the previous one left you in.</p><p>It saves a third of the space here. On an array of a thousand of these, that is 4 kB.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 3, q: "For a memory-mapped register struct, how do you handle an address gap?", o: ["Reorder the members so the gap is at the end", "Use __packed and compute offsets by hand", "Declare an explicit named reserved member", "Rely on the compiler to pad it correctly"], a: 2, why: "<p>The offsets in a register map are dictated by the silicon, so reordering points every field at the wrong register, and relying on padding makes the layout depend on a compiler decision that can change between targets or versions.</p><p>An explicit reserved member states the layout in the source: uint32_t RESERVED0; occupying the gap. Now the offsets are what you wrote rather than what the compiler chose.</p><p>Back it with _Static_assert on sizeof and offsetof for the important registers, so a change breaks the build rather than producing wrong addresses.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 3, q: "What is the most serious risk of __packed on a register map?", o: ["Slightly larger code, from byte-wise access", "Unaligned access, which can fault or split a word", "It makes the struct bigger than the register map", "It disables volatile on the packed members"], a: 1, why: "<p>Packing removes padding, so members land at unaligned addresses. Cortex-M0 faults on any unaligned word access, and every Cortex-M faults on LDRD, LDM and STM, which the compiler uses whenever it moves more than one word.</p><p>Worse, the compiler stops using single word loads and generates byte-wise sequences instead. On a read-to-clear register that clears the flags on the first byte read. On a FIFO data register it pops four times.</p><p>That last one is a correctness failure rather than a performance one, and it is why packing a register map is nearly always someone fighting padding they should have solved with reserved fields.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 2, q: "Why declare an 8-bit control register as uint32_t in a register struct?", o: ["To save space by matching the natural word", "The 4-byte spacing says it is a 32-bit register", "To force the next member onto an aligned offset", "Because uint8_t is not portable across compilers"], a: 1, why: "<p>The spacing in the datasheet tells you the access width. Registers listed at 0x00, 0x04, 0x08 are 32-bit registers, regardless of how many bits carry meaning.</p><p>Declaring one as uint8_t makes the compiler emit LDRB, a byte access. Many peripheral buses do not decode byte accesses at all and will return rubbish or ignore the write.</p><p>This is why vendor headers declare every register as uint32_t even when only two bits are used, and it is worth copying that convention.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 2, q: "Which assertion best protects a register map from drifting?", o: ["assert(sizeof(t) == 16) at run time in init", "_Static_assert on sizeof, plus offsetof checks", "A comment stating the expected size and offsets", "A unit test that reads the registers on target"], a: 1, why: "<p>A static assert is evaluated by the compiler, generates no code, cannot be disabled by NDEBUG, and a failing one never ships because the build does not exist.</p><p>sizeof alone catches the total drifting. offsetof on individual registers catches the more insidious case where the total is right but a field moved, which can happen when someone changes a type.</p><p>A runtime assert costs code and can abort in front of a customer. A comment protects nothing. A hardware test finds it late and only if the affected register is exercised.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 2, q: "Why must you never memcpy a struct onto a wire?", o: ["memcpy is too slow for a serial transmit path", "Padding is unspecified and the layout is not yours", "Structs cannot legally be cast to a byte pointer", "The receiver cannot allocate a matching struct"], a: 1, why: "<p>Three problems. Padding bytes have unspecified values, so you transmit leftover stack contents and the same call can send different bytes each time. The layout is a property of your compiler and target. The byte order is a property of your CPU.</p><p>None of those are properties of your protocol, which means the receiver has to guess, and a different compiler on either end breaks it.</p><p>Serialise field by field with explicit shifts. It costs a few lines and the wire format becomes something you defined rather than something you inherited.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 1, q: "Does b = a; work for structs in C?", o: ["No, struct assignment is not legal C; use memcpy", "Yes, it copies memberwise, arrays included", "Only for structs that contain no array members", "Only in C++, where operator= is generated"], a: 1, why: "<p>Struct assignment has been legal since C89 and copies every member, including array members.</p><p>That last part is notable, because bare arrays cannot be assigned: int x[4], y[4]; y = x; is illegal. Wrapping an array in a struct is occasionally used deliberately to make it assignable.</p><p>Two caveats: it is a shallow copy, so a pointer member is duplicated rather than what it points at, and it is not free, since a large struct means a large memcpy at every assignment or pass-by-value.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 3, q: "A struct containing a pointer is assigned to another struct. What is copied?", o: ["The pointer and everything it points at", "Only the pointer: both then share the memory", "Nothing: a struct holding a pointer cannot be assigned", "A reference back to the original struct"], a: 1, why: "<p>Struct assignment copies the bytes of each member. For a pointer member that means the address, not the pointee.</p><p>Afterwards both structs reference the same memory, so a write through one is visible through the other, and freeing through one leaves the other dangling.</p><p>If you need a deep copy you write it yourself, and that is worth documenting in the header so callers know which they are getting.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-structs", d: 2, q: "Why does passing a large struct by value matter on embedded?", o: ["It is undefined behaviour above a certain size", "Every call copies the whole struct onto the stack", "Structs larger than a word cannot be parameters", "It silently forces a heap allocation for the copy"], a: 1, why: "<p>A 68-byte struct passed by value is a 68-byte copy onto the stack for every call, and nothing in the call site suggests it. On a part with a 2 kB task stack and a few nested calls that adds up quickly.</p><p>Pass a pointer instead, and mark it const if the callee only reads, which documents the intent and lets the compiler check it.</p><p>The exception is a small struct of two or three words, which may be passed in registers and costs nothing.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-bits
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 1, q: "Which clears bit 5 without disturbing the others?", o: ["reg &= (1u << 5);", "reg |= ~(1u << 5);", "reg &= ~(1u << 5);", "reg ^= (1u << 5);"], a: 2, why: "<p>~(1u &lt;&lt; 5) is a mask with every bit set except bit 5. ANDing with it preserves every other bit and forces bit 5 to zero.</p><p>Option 1 ANDs with a mask that is zero everywhere except bit 5, so it clears everything else instead. Option 2 ORs with a mask that is one everywhere except bit 5, so it sets every other bit. Option 4 toggles, so it only clears bit 5 if it happened to be set.</p><p>The four idioms are worth memorising as a set: |= to set, &amp;= ~ to clear, ^= to toggle, &amp; to test.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 2, q: "if (status & READY == 0) actually evaluates what?", o: ["Whether the READY bit is clear in status", "status & 0, which is always false", "Whether status is zero, ignoring READY", "It is a syntax error and will not compile"], a: 1, why: "<p>== binds more tightly than &amp;, so the expression parses as status &amp; (READY == 0). READY is a non-zero mask, so the comparison yields 0, and status &amp; 0 is 0 for any status.</p><p>The condition is therefore always false and the block never executes, silently.</p><p>Ritchie acknowledged this precedence as a mistake, inherited from B where &amp; and &amp;&amp; were not distinguished. GCC warns under -Wall. The habit that removes it entirely is to parenthesise every bitwise operation without thinking about whether this one needs it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 2, q: "Why write 1u rather than 1 in a shift?", o: ["It is faster on an unsigned-only datapath", "Shifting into bit 31 of a signed int is UB", "It reduces code size by avoiding a sign extend", "Only unsigned values may be shifted at all"], a: 1, why: "<p>A bare 1 is a signed int. Left-shifting a signed value into or past the sign bit is undefined behaviour in C99 and C11, so 1 &lt;&lt; 31 has no defined meaning.</p><p>In practice it usually produces what you expect, right up until an optimiser decides the case cannot occur and removes the surrounding code.</p><p>Making the u a reflex means you never have to notice which bit you are shifting to. It costs one character and removes the class.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 2, q: "Write the value 5 into a 3-bit field at bits 6:4, leaving other bits alone.", o: ["reg |= (5u << 4);", "reg = (reg & ~(7u << 4)) | (5u << 4);", "reg = (reg & (7u << 4)) | (5u << 4);", "reg = (reg & ~(7u << 4)) | ((5u & 3u) << 4);"], a: 1, why: "<p>The clear-then-set idiom. ~(7u &lt;&lt; 4) clears the three-bit field while preserving everything else, then the OR writes the new value into the cleared space.</p><p>Option 1 can only set bits, never clear them, so a field currently holding 7 stays 7. Option 3 destroys every other bit in the register. Option 4 XORs, producing whatever the previous value happened to combine to.</p><p>Note this is a read-modify-write, so it is wrong on a read-to-clear or write-1-to-clear register.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 3, q: "Why include a mask in a field setter macro, as in ((x) << SHIFT) & MASK?", o: ["It makes the shift faster on some cores", "An out-of-range argument is truncated, not spilled", "It forces the arithmetic to be done unsigned", "C requires a mask whenever you shift a macro argument"], a: 1, why: "<p>Pass 9 into a 3-bit field and 9 &lt;&lt; 4 is 0x90, which sets bit 7 as well as the intended field. Bit 7 belongs to something else, and you have just changed it silently.</p><p>The mask confines the result to the field, so a bad argument produces a wrong value in the right place rather than corruption somewhere unrelated.</p><p>It is a small example of a general principle: where an interface can be misused, prefer making the misuse harmless or loud over documenting that it must not happen.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 2, q: "On a write-1-to-clear register, what does reg |= (1u << 3) risk?", o: ["Nothing: the OR only sets the bit you name", "Clearing every other flag that was set", "Setting all the bits in the register at once", "A bus fault, since the register is write-only"], a: 1, why: "<p>The read picks up the register's current contents, including any error or status flags that have latched. The OR adds your bit. The write puts that whole value back.</p><p>On a write-1-to-clear register, writing a 1 to a bit clears it. So every flag that was set is now cleared, and you have silently acknowledged errors that nothing handled.</p><p>The code looks entirely correct, which is what makes it dangerous. On those registers you write only the bits you intend to affect, with no read at all.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 2, q: "What does the access code RC mean in a register table?", o: ["Read only, and the value is cached by the bus", "Read to clear: reading clears the flags", "Reserved, and must not be accessed at all", "Read continuous: it updates while you read"], a: 1, why: "<p>The act of reading changes the register's state. Typically used for interrupt status registers, so that reading them acknowledges what you have seen.</p><p>The consequence is that a read-modify-write destroys every flag, and even a speculative read while debugging can lose an event.</p><p>It is one reason the access column exists and one reason to read it rather than only taking the address from the register table.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 1, q: "Why prefer a named constant over a raw bit number?", o: ["It is faster, since the name is a constant", "A wrong bit number is invisible to a reviewer", "It reduces code size in the final image", "Raw numbers are not portable between compilers"], a: 1, why: "<p>(1u &lt;&lt; 1) looks perfectly correct in isolation. Nothing in the code reveals that the author meant bit 2, so a reviewer has nothing to check it against and the error survives.</p><p>#define STATUS_READY (1u &lt;&lt; 2) with the datasheet table in a comment gives a reviewer something verifiable, and it puts the number in exactly one place.</p><p>It also makes the code readable: if (reg &amp; STATUS_READY) states intent, while if (reg &amp; 4) states an implementation.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 3, q: "Beyond concurrency, why can read-modify-write be wrong on hardware?", o: ["It is always safe if interrupts are disabled", "Some registers change state when read", "It is slower than a single direct write", "The compiler may reorder the read and write"], a: 1, why: "<p>The concurrency answer, that an interrupt can land between the read and the write, is the one most people give. The hardware answer is more specific to embedded.</p><p>Read-to-clear registers lose their flags on the read. Write-1-to-clear registers are cleared by writing back what you read. FIFO data registers pop a byte when read.</p><p>On all of those the read half of read-modify-write is destructive, so you write the value directly and only the bits you mean to affect. The datasheet's access column tells you which registers these are.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-bits", d: 2, q: "How do you test whether bit 2 is set?", o: ["if (reg == (1u << 2))", "if (reg & (1u << 2))", "if (reg >> 2)", "if (reg | (1u << 2))"], a: 1, why: "<p>ANDing with a single-bit mask isolates that bit and discards everything else, giving zero or non-zero.</p><p>Option 1 requires the whole register to equal 4, so it is false whenever any other bit is set. Option 3 is true if bit 2 or any higher bit is set. Option 4 ORs, which is always non-zero and also modifies nothing useful.</p><p>If you need a strict boolean, write !!(reg &amp; MASK) or compare against zero explicitly.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-isr
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 1, q: "Why must an ISR not block?", o: ["It uses too much stack while it is waiting", "Nothing that could unblock it can run", "Blocking calls are not implemented for ISRs", "It would trip the watchdog, but nothing worse"], a: 1, why: "<p>An interrupt handler runs at a priority above the application. Whatever would signal the thing you are waiting for, another task or a lower priority interrupt, cannot run until you return.</p><p>So the wait can never be satisfied. It is a deadlock that fires the first time the condition is not already true.</p><p>The correct structure is to take the minimum action, read the data register, push to a queue, set a flag, and return, letting a task do anything that might wait.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 2, q: "Give both reasons malloc is forbidden in an ISR.", o: ["It is slow and it uses too much stack", "Unbounded timing, and it is not reentrant", "It always fails, and it has been deprecated", "It disables interrupts, and it is slow"], a: 1, why: "<p>Unbounded timing: an allocator may walk free lists, coalesce blocks or split them, so the worst case depends on heap state and cannot be included in a latency budget.</p><p>Non-reentrancy: the heap's internal structures are modified in place. Interrupting an allocation and calling malloc again from the handler corrupts them, and the damage appears much later somewhere unrelated.</p><p>They are independent, so fixing one does not help. That is why the answer is not to use a faster allocator but not to allocate at all.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 3, q: "Does making malloc thread-safe with a mutex make it ISR-safe?", o: ["Yes, the mutex serialises every caller", "No: an ISR cannot block, so it deadlocks", "Only with a recursive mutex held by the ISR", "Only if the ISR and the task share a priority"], a: 1, why: "<p>A mutex protects the heap by making the second caller wait. Waiting is precisely what an interrupt handler must not do.</p><p>So if a task holds the heap lock when an interrupt fires and the handler allocates, the handler blocks on a lock that only the preempted task can release, and the task cannot run because the handler is holding the CPU.</p><p>You have converted intermittent heap corruption into a guaranteed deadlock. Neither is acceptable, which is why the rule is simply not to allocate in interrupt context.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 2, q: "Why does long work in an ISR matter if it finishes eventually?", o: ["It does not, provided that it finishes eventually", "It spends every equal or lower priority budget", "It increases average power consumption", "It fragments the heap the ISR allocates from"], a: 1, why: "<p>Interrupt latency is a global property of the system. While your handler runs, every interrupt at or below its priority is held off.</p><p>So a motor control handler that needed servicing within 50 microseconds now misses its deadline because an unrelated UART handler decided to parse a message. Nothing in the motor code changed and its timing guarantee is gone.</p><p>The phrase worth having is that you have spent someone else's latency budget without telling them.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 2, q: "A function with a static local is called from a task and an ISR. What is the problem?", o: ["It uses excess stack on the ISR's stack", "It is non-reentrant: the state is shared", "static is not allowed inside an ISR in C", "Nothing: static storage makes it safe"], a: 1, why: "<p>A function with only automatic locals is safe under concurrency because each call gets its own stack frame. A static local is shared by every caller.</p><p>So if an interrupt preempts the function halfway through and calls it again, the second call overwrites the state the first was relying on, and the first resumes with corrupted data.</p><p>It does not take two tasks: one task plus one interrupt is enough. This is why strtok, rand and printf are unsafe in an ISR, and why the reentrant variants such as strtok_r exist.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 1, q: "On Cortex-M, which priority preempts which?", o: ["Higher number preempts lower", "Lower number preempts higher", "They never preempt", "First to fire wins"], a: 1, why: "<p>ARM numbers priorities so that a lower number means more urgent. Priority 0 is the highest configurable priority and preempts priority 5.</p><p>This is genuinely counterintuitive and it catches people who assume larger means more important. It gets worse because only the upper bits of the priority field are implemented on most parts, so priorities 0 and 1 may be identical.</p><p>Check how many priority bits your part implements before designing a scheme around fine-grained levels.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 3, q: "A handler tests only the data-ready flag; an overrun flag latches unhandled. What happens?", o: ["Data is lost but the system carries on", "The interrupt re-asserts forever", "The peripheral resets itself after N overruns", "The flag clears on the next data read"], a: 1, why: "<p>The peripheral asserts its interrupt line while any enabled condition is pending. The overrun flag is one such condition and it has latched.</p><p>Your handler runs, tests only data-ready, finds it clear, does nothing and returns. The line is still asserted, so the handler is entered again immediately. The CPU never returns to any other work.</p><p>This is not a hang in your code, which is what makes it confusing: the handler is executing continuously and correctly. Always handle or at least clear the error conditions, and count them so the fault is visible.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 2, q: "Which is the best ranked first fix for an over-long ISR?", o: ["Raise its priority so it finishes sooner", "Lower the priority of every other interrupt", "Move the work to a task; the ISR queues it", "Increase the watchdog timeout to suit"], a: 2, why: "<p>Shortening the handler improves latency for every interrupt in the system, so it fixes the problem rather than moving it.</p><p>Raising or lowering priorities rescues one peripheral at the expense of others, because the long handler still blocks everything at or below its level. It is a stopgap, not a fix.</p><p>The full ranking is: shorten the ISR, restructure so the work happens in a task, use DMA so the interrupt disappears entirely, and only then reach for priority juggling.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 2, q: "Why is printf forbidden in an ISR?", o: ["It is fine if the UART is fast enough", "Slow, stack-hungry, non-reentrant, and blocks", "It only links when called from main", "It disables interrupts until the whole line is sent"], a: 1, why: "<p>Four independent reasons. It takes hundreds of microseconds to format and emit. It uses a surprising amount of stack for varargs and format parsing, and ISR stacks are often small. It typically holds internal buffers, making it non-reentrant. And most implementations block until the output device accepts the data.</p><p>Any one of those disqualifies it. Together they make it one of the most reliable ways to turn a working system into an unresponsive one.</p><p>If you must trace from an interrupt, use ITM, a GPIO toggle, or a circular log in RAM that a task drains later.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-isr", d: 3, q: "Which library functions are typically unsafe in an ISR for the same reason as a static local?", o: ["memcpy and memset, which touch shared memory", "strtok, rand and printf", "abs and labs, which use a static temporary", "All of them, since the library is not reentrant"], a: 1, why: "<p>All three hold state between calls: strtok remembers its position, rand holds a seed, printf holds buffers. Re-entering them corrupts the interrupted call's state.</p><p>memcpy, memset, abs and labs are pure functions of their arguments with no internal state, so they are reentrant and safe.</p><p>The general test is whether the function has any static or global state. Where a reentrant variant exists it is conventionally named with an _r suffix, as in strtok_r.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-concurrency
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 2, q: "A counter is written by an ISR and zeroed by main, and is volatile. Is that safe?", o: ["Yes, volatile is exactly what this needs", "No: two writers, and volatile is not atomic", "Only on 32-bit targets, where writes are atomic", "Only if the ISR runs at the highest priority"], a: 1, why: "<p>volatile guarantees each access is a real memory access in program order. It does nothing about what happens between two of your accesses.</p><p>With the ISR writing and main also writing, there is a window between main reading the value and main storing zero in which the ISR can increment. That increment is then lost.</p><p>The fixes are a critical section around the sequence, an atomic exchange, or a design where each side owns its own variable and neither writes the other's, which is what a single-producer single-consumer ring buffer does.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 3, q: "Two tasks talk to two DIFFERENT I2C devices via Arduino Wire and get each other's data. Why?", o: ["The bus cannot address two devices at once", "Wire holds one static buffer, so they overwrite", "The two devices share the same bus address", "Clock stretching by one device stalls the other one"], a: 1, why: "<p>Wire keeps a single static transmit buffer per instance, not one per device. beginTransmission resets it, write fills it, endTransmission sends it.</p><p>So if task A calls beginTransmission and write, then task B calls beginTransmission before A calls endTransmission, B has reset the buffer under A. A then transmits B's bytes to A's device.</p><p>The corruption happens entirely in library memory before a bit reaches the wire, which is why a logic analyser shows perfectly valid, correctly addressed transactions carrying the wrong payload. That observation is what identifies this class of fault.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 3, q: "Two tasks share one I2C sensor and get interleaved readings. The bus driver is thread-safe. Why does it still fail?", o: ["The driver is not actually thread-safe", "The device's address pointer is shared state", "The bus is too slow for two consumers", "Clock stretching corrupts the second transfer"], a: 1, why: "<p>Most I2C devices hold an internal address pointer. A register read is conventionally a write of the register number followed by a read from wherever the pointer now sits.</p><p>If those are two separate transactions, another task can address the same device and move the pointer in between. Both transactions were individually atomic and correctly locked, and the data is still wrong.</p><p>The fix is a combined transaction with a repeated START, which never releases the bus. Where a sequence genuinely spans several transactions, such as a read-modify-write of a config register, the driver needs its own lock.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 2, q: "In a ring buffer, head == tail is ambiguous. Which fix keeps it lock-free for one producer and one consumer?", o: ["Keep a shared count of items in the buffer", "Sacrifice one slot: full is (head+1)==tail", "Disable interrupts around every access", "Take a mutex in both producer and consumer"], a: 1, why: "<p>With a sacrificed slot, the producer writes only head and the consumer writes only tail. Neither ever writes the other's variable, so on a machine with atomic word writes no lock is required at all.</p><p>A shared count is written by both sides, which reintroduces exactly the shared mutable state you were trying to avoid, and therefore needs protecting.</p><p>The third option is free-running indices that never wrap, deriving the array offset by subtraction, which is what Zephyr's ring buffer does. That uses the whole buffer at the cost of half the index range.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 3, q: "Why does Zephyr's ring buffer use free-running indices that never wrap?", o: ["To save the RAM a separate count would need", "Empty and full become different values", "To allow more than one producer safely", "To keep both indices on their own cache line"], a: 1, why: "<p>If head and tail are wrapped into the array, head == tail means both empty and full and you cannot distinguish them. If they increment forever, head - tail gives the count directly, and empty is 0 while full is the buffer size.</p><p>Unsigned subtraction is modular, so the difference stays correct even after the counters wrap around, provided the buffer is at most half the index range. That constraint is why the maximum size is limited to half the type's range.</p><p>The array offset is then derived by subtraction from a base rather than by modulo, which also avoids a division inside an interrupt handler.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 2, q: "A clean analyser trace carries wrong data. What does that rule out?", o: ["A software fault somewhere above the driver", "A fault on the bus: electrical or protocol", "A bug in the bus driver's framing logic", "Nothing: a clean trace tells you very little"], a: 1, why: "<p>If the START, address, ACKs, data framing and STOP are all correct, the bus and both peripherals are doing their jobs. The wire faithfully carried what it was given.</p><p>So the wrong bytes were wrong before transmission, which means the fault is in software above the bus: the wrong buffer, the wrong values, or a shared library buffer overwritten by another context.</p><p>This is a genuinely useful diagnostic principle, because most engineers reach for the analyser, see nothing wrong, and get stuck. Knowing what a clean trace rules out is the skill.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 2, q: "Which design removes the need for a lock entirely?", o: ["Marking every shared variable volatile", "Disabling interrupts around every access", "One task owns the device and publishes", "Using a recursive mutex on the device"], a: 2, why: "<p>If exactly one piece of code touches the resource, there is no shared mutable state and therefore nothing to protect. Other tasks consume from a queue and never see the device.</p><p>This eliminates the class of bug rather than defending against it: no lock to forget, no ordering to get wrong, and only one place in the codebase that knows the device exists.</p><p>It also gives you a clear answer to why that task exists, which is worth having when someone asks about your task structure.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 1, q: "What is the correct question to ask about a concurrency bug?", o: ["Is this particular function thread-safe", "What is the shared state, and who owns it", "How many tasks touch the resource", "Are the task priorities the right way round"], a: 1, why: "<p>Thread safety is a property of a specific resource, not a general blessing. Asking whether something is thread-safe usually produces a yes that does not help.</p><p>The three candidates in embedded are: a library's internal buffer, the peripheral itself, and the device's own internal state. Each looks identical from outside, two tasks and wrong data, and each needs a different fix.</p><p>Identifying which one is shared is the whole diagnosis, and it is what turns 'I added a mutex and it worked' into an explanation you can defend.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 2, q: "get_message reads rx_len, memcpys, then sets rx_len to 0. What is lost?", o: ["Nothing: the memcpy is fast enough to be safe", "Bytes arriving mid-copy, and a torn message", "The whole buffer, since rx_len is zeroed", "Only the last byte written by the ISR"], a: 1, why: "<p>Two distinct faults in three lines. The ISR can fire during the memcpy and write into the buffer being copied, so the message tears. And any bytes that arrive after rx_len was read are destroyed when it is unconditionally set to zero.</p><p>Marking rx_len volatile does not help, because the problem is that there are two writers and the sequence is not atomic.</p><p>The fixes are a critical section around the three lines, or restructuring so that each side owns its own index, which is what a proper ring buffer does.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-concurrency", d: 3, q: "Which shared resource can disabling interrupts NOT protect you from?", o: ["An ISR", "A higher priority task", "A DMA controller", "A timer callback"], a: 2, why: "<p>DMA is a separate bus master. It moves data without executing any instructions and without the CPU's involvement, so masking interrupts has no effect on it whatsoever.</p><p>The other three all ultimately depend on the CPU running code, which interrupt masking does prevent.</p><p>Coordinating with DMA needs the controller's own completion mechanism, memory barriers so accesses are not reordered around the flag check, and cache maintenance on parts with a data cache.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-ub
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 2, q: "uint8_t f = 1; f << 31; is:", o: ["Fine: uint8_t is unsigned so the shift is too", "Undefined: f promotes to signed int first", "A compile error, since 31 exceeds the width", "Implementation-defined, like signed overflow"], a: 1, why: "<p>Integer promotion converts uint8_t to int, which is signed. The shift therefore happens on a signed value, and shifting into or past the sign bit is undefined in C99 and C11.</p><p>The unsignedness of the original type is lost at promotion and does not protect you. This surprises people because the declaration says unsigned.</p><p>It is the concrete reason behind the 1u habit: writing (uint32_t)f &lt;&lt; 31 or starting from an unsigned constant keeps the shift in unsigned arithmetic where it is defined.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 2, q: "Which of these is defined behaviour?", o: ["Signed integer overflow", "Unsigned integer overflow", "Shifting by the width of the type", "Dereferencing a null pointer"], a: 1, why: "<p>Unsigned arithmetic is defined to wrap modulo 2 to the power of the width. That is a guarantee you can rely on and build algorithms around.</p><p>Signed overflow is undefined, which is why optimisers may assume x + 1 &gt; x is always true. Shifting by the width or more is undefined. Null dereference is undefined.</p><p>The practical consequence is that timer and counter arithmetic must use unsigned types, because the wraparound trick depends on the defined behaviour.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 3, q: "Why is (get_ticks() - start < 1000) safe across a wrap but (get_ticks() < start + 1000) is not?", o: ["Subtraction is faster than addition here", "Unsigned subtraction is modular; the sum wraps", "Both are equally safe on unsigned counters", "The compiler special-cases known tick counters"], a: 1, why: "<p>Take start near the top of the range, say 0xFFFFFF00. In the addition form, start + 1000 overflows and wraps to 744. The condition becomes now &lt; 744, and now is about 4.29 billion, so it is false immediately and the loop does not wait at all.</p><p>In the subtraction form, once the counter wraps to say 100, 100 - 0xFFFFFF00 in unsigned 32-bit arithmetic gives 356, which is the true elapsed time.</p><p>It works because unsigned overflow is defined to wrap. The same code with a signed counter is undefined behaviour, so timer variables must be unsigned. The one limit is that the elapsed time must be less than the counter's full range.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 3, q: "What is wrong with int16_t v = *(int16_t *)buf; where buf is uint8_t[2]?", o: ["Nothing at all: the cast is well defined here", "Aliasing, alignment and byte order, all three", "It only fails on a big-endian machine", "It is slower than assembling with shifts"], a: 1, why: "<p>Three separate faults. Alignment: a uint8_t array is only guaranteed 1-byte aligned, and an unaligned 16-bit load faults on some cores. Endianness: it only works if the CPU's byte order happens to match the protocol's. Aliasing: C permits the compiler to assume objects of different types do not share memory.</p><p>The aliasing one is the subtle killer, because at -O2 the compiler may reorder or discard accesses based on an assumption you have broken, producing a bug that appears only when optimised.</p><p>Assembling with explicit shifts fixes all three at once, and memcpy is the sanctioned alternative when you genuinely need to reinterpret bytes.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 2, q: "Returning a pointer to a local buffer often appears to work. Why is that dangerous?", o: ["It only fails on a big-endian target", "The bytes survive until something overwrites", "The compiler always warns, so it is caught", "It never works; the read faults immediately"], a: 1, why: "<p>When the function returns, the stack pointer moves back but nothing erases the bytes. If the caller reads them before anything pushes a new frame over that region, the data is intact.</p><p>So it works in a simple test and fails later, when another call, a different optimisation, or an interrupt reuses the frame. On a microcontroller an interrupt uses the same stack and can land there at a moment you cannot predict.</p><p>It is undefined behaviour, so 'works' and 'does not work' are both permitted outcomes and neither is a guarantee. The fixes are a caller-supplied buffer, a string literal, or a static buffer with the reentrancy caveat that implies.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 1, q: "What does undefined behaviour license the compiler to do?", o: ["Emit a warning and continue compiling", "Assume it never happens, and optimise on that", "Insert a runtime check that traps at that point", "Fall back to -O0 for the enclosing function"], a: 1, why: "<p>The standard imposes no requirements at all, so the optimiser is entitled to reason that the situation cannot occur and to delete any code that would only be reached if it did.</p><p>This is why a null check placed after a dereference can be removed entirely: the dereference implies the pointer was not null, so the check is provably redundant.</p><p>It is also why UB bugs appear when optimisation is enabled, get worse as compilers improve, and cannot be reasoned about by imagining what the machine would do.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 2, q: "Which is the sanctioned way to reinterpret bytes as a wider type?", o: ["A pointer cast to the wider type", "A union with both members declared", "memcpy, which compilers recognise", "An inline assembly load instruction"], a: 2, why: "<p>memcpy has no alignment requirement and no aliasing problem, because it is specified in terms of copying bytes. Modern compilers recognise a fixed-size memcpy and generate the same instruction a direct load would, so there is no cost.</p><p>Type punning through a union is permitted in C, though not in C++, and is a defensible alternative in C code.</p><p>A pointer cast is the one to avoid, because it breaks the aliasing rules the optimiser relies on.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 2, q: "i = i++; is:", o: ["Well defined; i ends up unchanged", "Undefined: two unsequenced writes to i", "A constraint violation the compiler rejects", "Implementation-defined, so pick a compiler"], a: 1, why: "<p>The assignment writes i and the increment writes i, with no sequence point between them, which the standard declares undefined.</p><p>Different compilers and different optimisation levels genuinely produce different results, and none of them is wrong.</p><p>The wider rule: do not modify the same object more than once between sequence points, and do not both read and write it except to compute the value stored. GCC warns under -Wsequence-point.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 3, q: "Which tool best finds undefined behaviour in driver logic?", o: ["A logic analyser", "UBSan on a host build", "The linker map", "A stack high water mark"], a: 1, why: "<p>UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer instruments the code to detect signed overflow, out-of-range shifts, misaligned access, null dereference and more at run time, reporting the file and line.</p><p>It runs on host builds rather than on target, which is one more reason to keep driver logic free of platform dependencies so it can be compiled off-target at all.</p><p>The others cannot help: an analyser sees the bus, not the code; the map file shows placement; the high water mark measures stack. AddressSanitizer complements UBSan for memory errors.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-ub", d: 2, q: "A bug appears only at -O2. What are the two leading suspects?", o: ["Stack overflow and heap corruption", "Missing volatile and undefined behaviour", "Wrong linker script and bad clock", "Race conditions and priority inversion"], a: 1, why: "<p>Both are cases where the optimiser is permitted to assume something your code violates, and both are invisible at -O0 because nothing is optimised.</p><p>Missing volatile lets reads be hoisted and writes elided. Undefined behaviour lets whole branches be deleted.</p><p>The wrong response is to compile that file at -O0, which hides the fault. The right one is to check hardware registers and ISR-shared variables for volatile, then look for aliasing and signed overflow, then build for the host with UBSan.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-pointers
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 1, q: "int* a, b; declares:", o: ["Two pointers to int", "A pointer and an int", "Two plain ints", "A compile error under C11"], a: 1, why: "<p>The star is part of the declarator, not the type specifier. So int* a, b; declares a as a pointer to int and b as a plain int.</p><p>Writing the star next to the type suggests int* is a unit being applied to both, which is a C++ formatting habit that actively misleads in C.</p><p>Two conventions avoid it: put the star next to the name, as int *a, *b;, and declare one variable per line so the question cannot arise.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "void clear(uint8_t buf[64]) { memset(buf, 0, sizeof(buf)); } clears how many bytes?", o: ["64", "The size of a pointer", "0", "Undefined at compile time"], a: 1, why: "<p>An array parameter is always adjusted to a pointer. uint8_t buf[64] as a parameter is identical to uint8_t *buf, and the 64 is documentation the compiler discards.</p><p>So sizeof(buf) is the size of a pointer, typically 4 on a 32-bit target, and the function clears four bytes while reading as though it clears 64.</p><p>GCC catches this specific pattern under -Wall as -Wsizeof-pointer-memaccess. The general fix is that any function taking a buffer needs an explicit length parameter, because the size information is genuinely gone.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "const char *p versus char * const p:", o: ["Identical; const may sit either side", "Pointer to const char, versus const pointer", "The second form is not valid C at all", "Both stop the pointer being reassigned"], a: 1, why: "<p>Read outward from the identifier. In const char *p, the const is left of the star and qualifies the char, so you cannot write through the pointer but can repoint it. In char * const p, the const is right of the star and qualifies the pointer, so you can write through it but cannot repoint it.</p><p>The first is the common case for a function parameter you only read. The second is what you want for a fixed pointer to a hardware register.</p><p>const char * const p is both, which is the full form of a read-only register pointer.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "Which declares an array of 16 pointers to void(void) functions?", o: ["void *v[16](void);", "void (*v[16])(void);", "void *(*v)(void)[16];", "void v[16](*)(void);"], a: 1, why: "<p>The parentheses around *v[16] bind the star to the array elements, giving an array of pointers to functions.</p><p>Without them, void *v[16](void) parses as an array of functions returning void pointers, which C does not permit, because arrays of functions do not exist.</p><p>This is the declaration of a vector table, and in real startup code it would also be const and carry a section attribute so the linker places it at the reset address.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "Why does a callback API need a void *user parameter?", o: ["To let the callback return an error code", "So the callback knows which instance it serves", "To guarantee the alignment of the context", "C requires a void * in a function pointer"], a: 1, why: "<p>A bare function pointer carries no context. The only way for the callback to reach the state it needs is a global, which means you can have exactly one consumer.</p><p>With an opaque user pointer supplied at registration and passed back on every call, one callback function serves any number of instances and there are no globals.</p><p>This is the C idiom for a closure, and it is the same mechanism as the ctx field in an injected transport struct. Whenever you see void * as the last parameter of a callback, that is what it is for.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 1, q: "a[i] is defined as:", o: ["Only valid for arrays", "*(a + i)", "A special array operator", "Bounds-checked access"], a: 1, why: "<p>The subscript operator is defined in terms of pointer arithmetic, which is why it works identically on a pointer and on an array name.</p><p>An array name used in an expression decays to a pointer to its first element, so arr[3] and ptr[3] are the same operation.</p><p>A curiosity that follows: since *(a+i) equals *(i+a), the expression 3[arr] is legal C. Worth knowing as evidence of what the operator really is, not as something to write.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "What should every function that writes into a caller's buffer take?", o: ["A const qualifier", "A length parameter", "A return value", "A callback"], a: 1, why: "<p>Once an array crosses a function boundary it is a pointer, and the size information is gone. The callee has no way to discover how much room it was given.</p><p>Without a length, the contract is unstated and unchecked, and the first time a caller passes a smaller buffer you corrupt their memory with no diagnostic.</p><p>This is the single most repeated omission in driver APIs. An alternative for fixed sizes is a parameter declared as int16_t out[3], which documents the expectation even though the compiler still treats it as a pointer.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "Why mark a pointer parameter const when the function only reads through it?", o: ["It lets the compiler generate faster code", "A checked promise, and const data can be passed", "It reduces the stack used by the call", "It makes the function a candidate for inlining"], a: 1, why: "<p>Unlike a comment, the compiler enforces it: any attempt to write through the pointer is an error at the point it is written.</p><p>It also matters at the call site. A caller holding a const buffer, such as a table in flash, cannot pass it to a non-const parameter without casting the qualifier away, and casting away const is exactly the habit you do not want to teach your users.</p><p>Occasionally it enables optimisation, but the value is in the documented and checked contract rather than in generated code.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 3, q: "Which storage duration makes a function non-reentrant if you return a pointer to it?", o: ["Automatic", "Static", "Allocated", "Register"], a: 1, why: "<p>Returning a pointer to a static buffer is safe from a lifetime point of view, because the buffer outlives the call. But every caller receives a pointer to the same memory.</p><p>So two tasks, or a task and an interrupt, overwrite each other's results, and each sees data it did not produce.</p><p>Returning a pointer to an automatic is worse, because the memory is invalid immediately. Returning allocated memory is safe but transfers an ownership obligation that must be documented. The cleanest option is usually a caller-supplied buffer.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-pointers", d: 2, q: "You pass &local to a task that outlives the function. What is the fault?", o: ["Nothing, if the task is high enough priority", "The frame is gone; the task reads dead memory", "The compiler copies the value into the task", "It only fails where pointers are 64 bits"], a: 1, why: "<p>The address refers to a stack frame that is released when the creating function returns. The task then reads memory that belongs to whatever ran next.</p><p>It frequently appears to work, because a newly created high-priority task often runs and copies the value before the creating function returns. Change a priority or add a line after the create call and it breaks.</p><p>The fixes are to make the variable static, to pass the value itself through the void pointer if it fits, or to allocate it. This is the classic RTOS beginner bug.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-preproc
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "#define SQUARE(x) x * x, then SQUARE(2 + 3) gives:", o: ["25", "11", "10", "A compile error"], a: 1, why: "<p>The preprocessor performs text substitution with no understanding of precedence, so SQUARE(2 + 3) expands to 2 + 3 * 2 + 3. Multiplication binds tighter, giving 2 + 6 + 3, which is 11.</p><p>Two rules fix it, and both are needed: parenthesise every parameter, and parenthesise the whole body. ((x) * (x)) expands to ((2 + 3) * (2 + 3)) and gives 25.</p><p>Apply both without deciding whether this particular macro needs them, because the one you skip is the one that bites.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "x = MAX(i++, j); with a typical MAX macro. What happens to i?", o: ["Incremented once, as written", "Incremented twice: it appears twice", "Not incremented, since the macro takes text", "Undefined behaviour, so anything may happen"], a: 1, why: "<p>MAX(a,b) is typically ((a) &gt; (b) ? (a) : (b)), so the argument text appears twice. Substituting i++ gives ((i++) &gt; (j) ? (i++) : (j)).</p><p>The comparison evaluates i++ once, yielding 5 and leaving i at 6. The true branch evaluates it again, yielding 6 and leaving i at 7. So x gets 6, not the 5 you compared, and i ends at 7.</p><p>Any macro that uses a parameter more than once is unsafe with any argument that has a side effect, and the answer is a static inline function rather than a cleverer macro.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 3, q: "Why is x = MAX(read_status(), 10); worse on embedded than the i++ version?", o: ["It is not worse; both are the same fault", "It reads the hardware register twice", "It is slower, because of the extra call", "It allocates a temporary on every call"], a: 1, why: "<p>The double evaluation reaches hardware rather than just a variable. Two reads of a status register are two bus transactions.</p><p>On a read-to-clear register the first read clears the flags, so the second returns a different value and the event is lost. On a FIFO data register you pop two bytes and discard one.</p><p>The i++ version corrupts a local. This version silently loses hardware events, which is far harder to notice and far harder to trace back to a macro.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "What is wrong with #define ERR_BASE = 0x8000; ?", o: ["Nothing; the equals sign is optional", "A define is text: no equals, no semicolon", "The value is too large for a macro", "It needs parentheses around the value"], a: 1, why: "<p>#define NAME replacement substitutes everything after the name. Here the replacement text is literally '= 0x8000;'.</p><p>So if (err == ERR_BASE) expands to if (err == = 0x8000;), which is a syntax error, and the diagnostic points at the use site rather than at the definition, which is what makes it confusing.</p><p>The correct form is #define ERR_BASE 0x8000, and anything derived from it should be parenthesised: #define ERR_WRONG_PART (ERR_BASE + 1).</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "static int counter = 0; in a header included by three .c files gives:", o: ["One counter, shared by all three files", "Three separate counters, one per file", "A duplicate symbol error at link time", "A compile error on the second include"], a: 1, why: "<p>#include is a paste, so each translation unit gets its own definition, and static gives each one internal linkage. The result is three independent variables that happen to share a name.</p><p>The insidious part is that static suppresses the duplicate-symbol link error that would otherwise have caught it. Without static you would be told immediately.</p><p>To share one variable, put extern int counter; in the header and int counter = 0; in exactly one .c file. Better still, keep the variable static inside the one file that owns it and expose a function.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "Why should each .c file include its own header first?", o: ["It measurably speeds up compilation", "It proves the header is self-contained", "The C standard requires that ordering", "It removes the need for any include guard"], a: 1, why: "<p>If the header is first, nothing else has been included yet, so any type or macro it uses without including must come from itself. A missing include is exposed immediately.</p><p>Put it last and the header may compile only because the file happened to include something else beforehand, and it breaks months later when someone includes it somewhere new.</p><p>There is a second benefit: an unused header is never compiled at all, so it can contain arbitrary nonsense. Including it from its own .c is what makes it real code.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "#ifdef X versus #if X:", o: ["Identical; #if is just the newer spelling", "ifdef tests existence; if evaluates the value", "#if only works when the macro is an integer", "#ifdef has been deprecated since C99"], a: 1, why: "<p>#ifdef X tests only for the existence of the macro, so it is true even if X is defined as 0. #if X evaluates the replacement text as an integer expression.</p><p>The difference matters in configuration systems that define options as 0 rather than leaving them undefined. Kconfig-style systems define booleans as 1 or omit them, so both forms happen to work there.</p><p>A related trap: an undefined identifier inside #if evaluates as 0 rather than producing an error, so a typo silently disables a block. Enable -Wundef to catch it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 1, q: "Preferred replacement for a function-like macro?", o: ["An inline assembly block", "A static inline function", "A global variable holding the result", "A goto into a shared block"], a: 1, why: "<p>An inline function evaluates each argument exactly once, is type checked, respects scope, can be stepped in a debugger, and produces the same code because the compiler inlines it.</p><p>Every problem with function-like macros comes from the fact that they are text: double evaluation, precedence surprises, no type checking, and unreadable errors.</p><p>Function-like macros are largely a habit from before inline existed. Keep them only for the few things a function genuinely cannot do.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 3, q: "Which case genuinely still needs a macro rather than an inline function?", o: ["Computing a maximum of two values", "Stringifying, token pasting, array sizes", "Anything on a performance-critical path", "Anything that takes a pointer argument"], a: 1, why: "<p>The # operator turns an argument into a string literal and ## pastes tokens together. Neither has any function equivalent, because they operate on source text before compilation.</p><p>Integer constant expressions are the other case: an array size, a case label or a static assert condition must be a constant expression, and a function call is not one even if the compiler could fold it.</p><p>A third is any wrapper that needs __FILE__ and __LINE__ from the call site rather than from its own definition, which is how assertion and logging macros work.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-preproc", d: 2, q: "Why put an extern \"C\" guard in a C header?", o: ["To enable C99 features in a C++ build", "So C++ links against unmangled C symbols", "To force the functions to be inlined", "To suppress warnings about C constructs"], a: 1, why: "<p>C++ encodes parameter types into symbol names so that overloading works. A C++ file including a plain C header would therefore look for a mangled symbol that the C compiler never produced, and the link fails.</p><p>extern \"C\" tells the C++ compiler to use C linkage for those declarations. The #ifdef __cplusplus around it is needed because a C compiler does not understand the syntax.</p><p>It costs four lines and it is the difference between your driver being usable from C++ or not. Reviewers notice its absence.</p>" },

// ---------------------------------------------------------- emb-serial
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 1, q: "In little-endian, 0x12345678 stored at 0x100 puts which byte at 0x100?", o: ["0x12", "0x34", "0x56", "0x78"], a: 3, why: "<p>Little-endian places the least significant byte at the lowest address, so 0x100 holds 0x78, 0x101 holds 0x56, and so on.</p><p>ARM and x86 are both little-endian in practice, while network protocols are traditionally big-endian, which is where the term network byte order comes from.</p><p>The important habit is to treat byte order as a property of the protocol and state it explicitly in code, rather than inheriting whatever the CPU happens to do.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 2, q: "Why assemble multi-byte values with explicit shifts rather than a pointer cast?", o: ["It is faster than a single pointer load", "Any byte order, no alignment, no aliasing", "Pointer casts of that kind are not legal C", "It uses less stack than a cast would"], a: 1, why: "<p>One line fixes three separate problems. The shifts state the byte order explicitly, so the code is correct on a big-endian target too. Byte reads have no alignment requirement. And you only ever access uint8_t through uint8_t, so the strict aliasing rules are respected.</p><p>The pointer cast has all three faults at once, and the aliasing one is the least visible: it produces a bug that appears only at -O2.</p><p>Compilers recognise the shift idiom and generate efficient code, so there is no performance argument for the cast.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 2, q: "What is the most serious problem with sending a struct down a UART?", o: ["It wastes bandwidth on the padding bytes", "Padding is unspecified, so it can leak memory", "The receiver has no way to parse the bytes", "It is too slow for a byte-oriented link"], a: 1, why: "<p>Padding bytes have unspecified values. On automatic storage that means whatever was previously on the stack, so the same call can transmit different bytes on successive invocations.</p><p>That makes the output non-deterministic, which breaks any test that compares frames, and in a security context it is an information leak of memory contents.</p><p>The layout and byte order problems are real too, but they are at least consistent for a given build. Unspecified padding is not even that.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 2, q: "Which framing method resynchronises naturally after a lost byte?", o: ["Fixed length frames", "A length prefix", "A delimiter with escaping", "None of them recover without a timeout"], a: 2, why: "<p>A unique delimiter byte can always be found again by scanning forward, so a receiver that loses its place recovers at the next frame boundary without any timeout.</p><p>Fixed length and length prefix both depend on knowing where you are. Lose a byte and every subsequent frame is misaligned until something resets the state, usually an idle timeout.</p><p>The cost of a delimiter is escaping any occurrence of it inside the payload, which expands the data. COBS is the standard encoding that does this with a bounded and predictable overhead.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 3, q: "What is the risk of a length-prefixed frame?", o: ["It cannot carry arbitrary binary data", "A corrupted length consumes the wrong count", "It is inefficient for very short frames", "It still needs escaping of the length byte"], a: 1, why: "<p>The length field is data like any other and can be corrupted in transit. If it is read as 60000 instead of 12, the receiver waits for bytes that never come, or consumes the next several frames as payload.</p><p>So validate it before trusting it: against a maximum frame size, and ideally against what the protocol allows for that message type.</p><p>Combine that with a timeout so a receiver waiting on a bogus length eventually gives up and resynchronises rather than hanging forever.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 2, q: "Why prefer a CRC over an additive checksum?", o: ["A CRC is faster to compute in software", "It catches transpositions and burst errors", "A CRC is shorter for the same coverage", "A CRC also authenticates the sender"], a: 1, why: "<p>A simple sum is blind to reordering, because addition is commutative, and it misses many multi-bit error patterns. Two bytes swapped produce the same checksum.</p><p>A CRC is division in a polynomial field, chosen so that common physical error patterns, particularly bursts, are guaranteed detectable up to a known length.</p><p>For the same field width you get far better detection, and the computation is cheap with a lookup table or a hardware unit. It is not slower in any way that matters.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 3, q: "Does a CRC protect against deliberate tampering?", o: ["Yes, if the CRC is over the whole frame", "No: an attacker just recomputes it", "Only CRC-32, which is long enough", "Only if the polynomial is kept secret"], a: 1, why: "<p>A CRC is a public, keyless function. Anyone who can modify the message can recompute the CRC over the modified message, and the result is indistinguishable from a valid frame.</p><p>Keeping the polynomial secret does not help, because it can be recovered from a handful of known message and CRC pairs.</p><p>Integrity against an adversary requires a keyed construction such as an HMAC, or a signature if the receiver must verify origin without holding the secret. A CRC is an error-detection code, not a security primitive.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 2, q: "Two implementations of the same CRC disagree. What is the most likely cause?", o: ["The two machines differ in endianness", "Coverage, init value, reflection or final XOR", "A hardware fault in one CRC unit", "The two were built by different compilers"], a: 1, why: "<p>A CRC is defined by more than its polynomial: the initial register value, whether input bytes are bit-reflected, whether the output is reflected, the final XOR value, and exactly which bytes are covered.</p><p>Two implementations calling themselves CRC-16 can differ in every one of those and both be correct by their own definition.</p><p>So specify all of it in the protocol document, and include a worked example frame in hex with its CRC. That single example resolves more integration disputes than any amount of prose.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 2, q: "Why include a version byte early in a wire protocol?", o: ["It keeps the header a round number of bytes", "The format can change without breaking devices", "It improves the CRC's error detection", "Most wire protocol standards mandate one"], a: 1, why: "<p>Without it, every format change is a breaking change, and a fleet with mixed firmware versions becomes impossible to support.</p><p>With it, a receiver can decide whether it understands the frame, fall back to older handling, or reject it cleanly with a useful message rather than misparsing it.</p><p>Put it early, before anything variable, so a parser can read it without first understanding the rest of the frame. And define what a receiver does with an unknown version before you need it.</p>" },
{ t: "emb-serial", d: 1, q: "In a UART frame, which data bit is transmitted first?", o: ["Most significant", "Least significant", "Parity", "It depends on the baud rate"], a: 1, why: "<p>The frame is a start bit low, then data bits least significant first, then optional parity, then a stop bit high. The line idles high, so the falling edge of the start bit is what the receiver detects.</p><p>Knowing the order matters when reading a raw capture: if you are decoding a waveform by eye, reading the bits in the wrong direction gives you a byte-reversed value.</p><p>It also explains why an off-by-one bit alignment shows up as every byte shifted, with the start bit appearing as data bit 0.</p>" }

];
